WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:13.220
Hi, I'm Mayuko, I'm a Software Engineer
living in the San Francisco Bay Area

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:18.360
AKA Silicon Valley and I make youtube
videos about my life as a Software Engineer!

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.680
We need software engineers now more than ever,

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.250
to push forward the technological
bounds to unlock the potential of

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:28.690
humanity and improve society. We're using technology to communicate across

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:32.949
geographical boundaries with loved ones;
we've been reexamining ourselves in how

00:00:32.949 --> 00:00:36.940
we relay credible and factual
information for everything from local to

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:41.260
global scale news. And people are living
longer thanks to advances in medical

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.519
technology and countless lives are being
saved from technology that has been made

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.929
to be more readily accessible. And due to
the great potential of technology,

00:00:49.929 --> 00:00:54.720
the tech industry is booming but studies
have shown that there aren't nearly

00:00:54.729 --> 00:00:59.379
enough software engineers to fill the
demand of the industry. There has been a

00:00:59.379 --> 00:01:03.879
huge effort on educating more folks on
learning how to code to fill this gap.

00:01:03.879 --> 00:01:08.440
There are tons of amazing tutorials and
books out there about the technical

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:13.120
aspects of being a software engineer, so
I wanted to make one that talks about

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.160
the more nuanced, human aspects of
succeeding as a software engineer.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.320
This new video series is called How to Kickstart Your Career in Software Engineering.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.760
This series is designed for
you folks who are just starting out in

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.280
your career; maybe you just graduated
from a bootcamp and you're about to

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.390
start your first full time role, or maybe
you just finished a degree and you're

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:38.200
stepping into the industry as a
full-timer. Starting a new career can be

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.400
super scary though and I want you to
feel confident and supported in the

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.660
first couple of months at your new job.
 So in this series, I'm going to guide you

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:50.170
from your first day on the job and then
help you navigate the waters through the

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.950
first few months of your job. We'll do
this by talking about how to have a

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:58.120
fruitful relationship with other people
on your team, by exploring the world of

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:02.800
networking, and by understanding how to
have a healthy relationship with your work.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:06.280
As a software engineer for the last
five years, I've used the tips we're

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.149
going to talk about every new job I
start. Of course, how you apply these

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:13.719
tips will differ based on the type of
software engineering job and what part

00:02:13.719 --> 00:02:17.900
of the industry you work in. However this
series is designed for a general

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:22.000
software engineering audience so I hope
that you find most of the content useful.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.180
Now, it's honestly not an understatement
that y'all are going to play a key part

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:32.150
in changing the world. You have SO much
say and what gets made and how it gets

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:38.060
made and I want you to do so well and
feel empowered to be great! So I hope you

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:42.020
like this series and without further ado,
Let's freaking do this.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.360
you

